---------------------------------------------
| INSTALLING THE LIBRARY FOR EXTERNAL USAGE |
---------------------------------------------

The Makefile in src/ provides all that is needed to build the library. For
testing purposes, you can run

$ make [gnu|intel|pgi|nag|cray]

to build with a specific compiler using pre-set compiler flags. However, if
you want to build MARBL to link with an outside program, you may want more
control over the compile-time options. To that end, run

$ make OBJ_DIR=[location of object files] depends
$ make [desired_location_of_lib]/libmarbl.a \
       USE_DEPS=TRUE \
       FC=[compiler] \
       FCFLAGS="[compiler flags]" \
       OBJ_DIR=[location of object files] \
       INC_DIR=[location of .mod files]

The first call will produce the dependency file (shared_deps.d), the second call
will build libmarbl.a and the *.mod files. When building the GCM, you will need
to add

-I$(INC_DIR)

And when linking

-L$(LIB_DIR) -lmarbl

-----------------------------------
| BUILDING THE LIBRARY WITH CMAKE |
-----------------------------------

MARBL can also be built with CMake 3.24 or newer, in a separate build
directory (in-source builds would overwrite the Makefiles, so they are refused):

$ cmake -S . -B build [-D<option>=<value> ...]
$ cmake --build build
$ cmake --install build --prefix [install location]

To choose the compiler, set FC for the first configure (e.g. FC=ifx cmake ...)
or pass -DCMAKE_Fortran_COMPILER=[compiler].

The equivalent of make clean is

$ cmake --build build --target clean

which removes the object files, *.mod files, libmarbl.a and the driver, but
keeps the configuration: the compiler and the options below. To start over,
e.g. with another compiler, delete the build directory instead:

$ rm -rf build

This installs libmarbl.a in [prefix]/lib (lib64 or lib/[multiarch] on some
systems), the *.mod files in [prefix]/include/marbl, and a CMake package, so
another CMake project can use

find_package(MARBL REQUIRED)
target_link_libraries(<target> PRIVATE MARBL::marbl)

A CMake project can instead build MARBL as part of its own build with
add_subdirectory(); by default MARBL then adds none of its own compiler flags
and does not build the stand-alone driver.

Options:

MARBL_USE_MPI            Use MPI_Wtime() for MARBL's timers; the driver is then
                         marbl-mpi.exe, and the installed package requires MPI
                         (default: OFF)
MARBL_USE_DEFAULT_FLAGS  Add the per-compiler flags from the Makefiles
                         (default: ON when MARBL is the top-level project)
MARBL_BUILD_DRIVER       Build the stand-alone driver in build/driver_exe
                         (default: ON when MARBL is the top-level project)
MARBL_DRIVER_NETCDF      Build the driver with netCDF output; netCDF-Fortran
                         is found with pkg-config (default: OFF)
MARBL_INSTALL            Generate install rules (default: ON)
CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE         Build type for single-configuration generators such
                         as Unix Makefiles or Ninja (default: RelWithDebInfo,
                         which is -O2 -g with gfortran and ifx, when MARBL is
                         the top-level project)
CMAKE_COMPILE_WARNING_AS_ERROR
                         Treat warnings as errors, as the gnu targets in the
                         Makefiles do; MARBL does not yet compile without
                         warnings under ifx (default: OFF)

The last two are CMake's own variables rather than MARBL options.

The test suite in tests/ still builds with the Makefiles.
